@startmindmap exp5_map
title Experiment 5 - map: thread scaling, one shared counter vs sharded
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF

* Exp 5: thread-count scaling
** Question
*** As threads are added, how does total throughput change for each strategy?
*** Does sharing or atomicity cause the drop?
** Setup
*** 1 to 8 threads, each on a different physical core (no SMT siblings)
*** 2 M increments per thread, 11 trials per point, order rotating
*** Five contenders: fetch_add, CAS loop, spinlock, mutex, sharded
** Sharded = the fix
*** Each thread fetch_adds its OWN padded line
*** Still one atomic per increment, but nothing is shared
** Results, total M increments per second
*** fetch_add: 707 at 1 thread, 237 at 2, 155 at 8
*** CAS loop: 570 at 1, 94 at 2, 67 at 8
*** spinlock: 640 at 1, 89 at 2, 12 at 8
*** mutex: 234 at 1, 84 at 2, 52 at 8
*** sharded: 690 at 1, 1380 at 2, 5211 at 8, about 7.6 times the 1 thread rate
** Lesson
*** Every shared strategy goes DOWN as threads are added
*** Sharing is the cost, not the atomic instruction
*** The spinlock collapses, the mutex degrades into the kernel
** Open question
*** Cores 6 to 8 were faster than cores 1 to 5, possibly the chiplet boundary
** Concepts to read first
*** Concept 07: cache line ping-pong
*** Concept 09: sharding
@endmindmap
